A high-pressure cold gas spray system 5/11 (Impact Innovations GmbH, Germany) was used for the coatings experiments equipped with a powder feeder system. The system is designed to operate at high pressures of up to 50 bars and temperatures reaching 1100 °C. Nitrogen was utilized as both the powder carrier gas and the process gas. The CGS gun is equipped with an integrated heating element with a nominal heat output of up to 44 kW and supported by a water-cooling system of the nozzle to reduce clogging effects. To perform the handling operations for the coating process, the spray gun is mounted on an IRB 4600 industrial robot (ABB Ltd, Switzerland). For the tubular coating tensile (TCT) tests, a mobile rotating device (lathe) with a three-jaw chuck is used. It allows the coating of cylindrical specimens with the ability to control the rotation speed of the attached specimens. To analyze the behavior of coated surface, a “One Factor at a Time (OFAT)” experimental design was used. This OFAT design, presented in Table  1 , involved three sets of variables, namely gun velocity (GV), standoff distance (SD), and spray angle (SA). A simple approximation of number of passes was made for each iteration to achieve a similar range of coating thickness. Table 1 Spray parameters for the OFAT study Spray parameters (constant) Pressure 50  bar \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
