As the spray angle decreases from Sa = 90° to 70° and subsequently to 50°, there is a noticeable decline in both the thickness and adhesion of the coating to the substrate. However, the microhardness of the coatings remained unchanged, whereas a slight decrease in porosity was observed when spraying at lower angles. The coating structure remains intact and exhibits minimal defects at a spray angle of SA = 70°. However, reducing the angle to SA = 50° during cold gas spraying leads to the formation of significant cracks and delamination of the coating, as observed after coating for the TCT test. The spray angle therefore significantly influenced the tensile strength of the coating. While highest tensile strength was obtained for the sample sprayed with 90° (651 ± 42.9 MPa), medium strength of 491 ± 70.8 MPa was determined for the specimen sprayed with SA = 70°, and the lowest value of 142 ± 100 MPa along with a strong scattering of the values (i.e., highest standard deviation) was found for the sample sprayed with lowest SA = 50°. At a spray angle of 50°, significant bulges or protrusions of larger quantities of coating material with irregular shapes were observed after coating for the TCT test, which subsequently delaminated from the substrate. Additionally, vertical cracks in the coating, perpendicular to the surface, were identified. These issues are attributed to poor particle bonding, resulting from insufficient adiabatic shearing processes and the presence of compressive residual stress. The standoff distance (20 to 60 mm) had no relevant influence on the coating properties, while increased gun velocities (10 to 30 m/min) showed a decrease in deposition rate of the coating. The reduction in spraying angle (SA = 90° to 50°) led to severe coating defects and low deposition rates. The powder morphology and phases are mainly preserved in the coating microstructure after cold spraying without the formation of oxides. Coatings applied with varying spray angles were subsequently analyzed for their sliding wear performance and friction properties. The primary wear mechanism identified was abrasion. This effect is enhanced by the successive enrichment of aluminum oxide debris in the wear track derived from material removal from the alumina ball during to cyclically repeated contact at the interface between the coating surface and the ball. It is assumed that the eggshell effect, as proposed by Arieta et al. (Ref  27 ), enhances the abrasion, because the harder alumina-enriched zone cannot deform in the same manner as the softer underlying (subsurface) region, resulting in crack formation and material breakages of the IN718 coating. The primary wear mechanism identified in the study was abrasion. This effect is intensified by the progressive enrichment of aluminum oxide particles in the boundary region between the IN718 coating surface and the alumina ball. This enrichment occurs due to material removal from the alumina ball during repetitive cyclic contact, resulting in an accumulation of debris within the wear track. It is hypothesized that the eggshell effect, as proposed by Arieta et al. (Ref  27 ), further enhances abrasion. This is because the harder alumina-enriched zone is unable to deform in the same manner as the softer underlying subsurface region, leading to crack formation and material breakage of the IN718 coating. The coating displays a moderate frictional coefficient of 0.72 with an average sliding wear coefficient of  3.5 · 10 - 4 mm 3 /Nm \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
